## Part 1: Section 4 - GASC System Architecture
Target Track: ICML (System Optimization & Data-Centric AI)
This section provides the formal academic documentation for the Governance-Aware Semantic Compiler (GASC), outlining its programmatic compilation mechanisms and metadata pruning engine.
## 4.1 Architectural Overview
The primary objective of the Governance-Aware Semantic Compiler (GASC) is to decouple enterprise data governance enforcement from declarative prompt compilation pipelines. Traditional multi-agent systems suffer from context inflation and token-induced latency because they statically inject comprehensive database schemas, business metrics, and access policies into every agent prompt. GASC resolves this bottleneck by treating the entire enterprise metadata registry as a high-dimensional semantic search array, using a two-stage compilation pipeline: Semantic Pruning and Compliance-Safe Program Generation.

                           [ Enterprise Metadata Catalog ]
                                         |
                                         v
[ Ad-hoc User Query ] ---> [ Stage 1: Semantic Pruner ] ---> [ Sparse Schema Sub-Graph ]
                                                                        |
                                                                        v
[ Enforced System Policies ] -------------------------------> [ Stage 2: Program Compiler ]
                                                                        |
                                                                        v
                                                             [ Verified Safe SQL/PySpark ]

## 4.2 Stage 1: Semantic Metadata Pruning
Let $\mathcal{C}$ represent the global lakehouse catalog dataset containing a set of tables $\{T_1, T_2, \dots, T_m\}$, and let $\mathcal{P}$ represent the set of active corporate security policies (e.g., Row-Level Security tags, Column-Level masking constraints). When an ad-hoc natural language query $\mathcal{Q}$ is initialized by a user, it is first routed into the GASC Pruning Module.
Instead of parsing the raw string payload of the catalog linearly, GASC computes an implicit dependency matrix between the tokens of $\mathcal{Q}$ and the semantic definitions of $\mathcal{C}$. Tables and attributes that yield an analytical relevance score below a pre-configured threshold $\tau$ are dynamically pruned from the context window.
The output of this stage is a minimal, dense schema sub-graph $\mathcal{C}_{pruned} \subset \mathcal{C}$ that contains only the technical schema definitions absolutely required to satisfy the user's intent.
## 4.3 Stage 2: Compliance-Safe Program Compilation
The compressed schema sub-graph $\mathcal{C}_{pruned}$ is passed directly to the GASC Compilation Engine alongside the active policy constraints $\mathcal{P}$. Rather than relying on simple text modifications, GASC utilizes DSPy programmatic signatures to compile the declarative request into concrete, executable relational database code (such as ANSI SQL or PySpark scripts).
During compilation, active security policies are converted into explicit database filtering commands (such as WHERE clause additions or SHA256 column masks) that are appended directly to the output abstract syntax tree (AST). This architecture ensures that even if an underlying foundation model attempts to access a restricted data cell, the compiled code structurally prevents data exposure.
By offloading metadata search to a sparse matrix optimization step, GASC significantly reduces input token footprints while guaranteeing strict runtime safety enforcement.
